E("es-u2-cr-q08",2,["EA12.2.4"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Use the diagram below to answer the following questions. (i) List the rock units in order from oldest to youngest. (ii) Identify the feature labelled K. (iii) Identify the oldest igneous rock unit in the diagram, and explain your choice.",["(i) J, I, H, F, D, E, K, C, B, A, G. (ii) K is an angular unconformity. (iii) E is the oldest igneous unit, because G cuts E and contact-metamorphoses it."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) Oldest to youngest: J, I, H, F, D, E, K, C, B, A, G. The tilted set (J up to D) was deposited first, then the granite E intruded; K is the erosion surface cut across them; the flat set C, B, A was deposited on K; the dyke G cuts everything, so it is youngest. (ii) K is an angular unconformity. (iii) E is the oldest igneous unit: the dyke G cuts across E (cross-cutting relationships), and contact metamorphism from G affected E, so E existed first.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the J-I-H-F-D-E block in the correct order in part (i).<br>- 1 mark: the K-C-B-A-G block in the correct order in part (i).<br>- 1 mark: K identified as an angular unconformity.<br>- 1 mark: E identified as the oldest igneous unit, with the cross-cutting or contact-metamorphism reasoning.<br><br>Diagram key: A conglomerate, B sandstone, C shale, D limestone, F sandstone, H conglomerate, I coal, J sandstone, E granite, G basalt.",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u2-24-jun07-63g.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Work the tilted set first, then the surface K, then the flat set and the dyke."]);

