E("es-u2-cr-q10",2,["EA12.2.4"],3,"var",varset([function(){var q=textq("Use the diagram below to answer the following questions. (i) List the rock units in order from oldest to youngest. (ii) Identify the feature found in rock unit M. (iii) Explain how the feature in rock unit M was formed.",["(i) Q, O, N, L, M, P, H, I, J, K. (ii) Inclusions (included fragments of older rock). (iii) When M intruded, pieces of the surrounding units N and L broke off and were enclosed in M as it solidified."],"<b>Model answer:</b> (i) Oldest to youngest: Q, O, N, L, M, P, H, I, J, K. The sedimentary units were deposited in order; M intrudes L and N; P, H, I and J were deposited above; K is the youngest intrusion. (ii) The feature in M is inclusions: fragments of other rock enclosed within it. (iii) As the magma of M forced its way through units N and L, fragments of those rocks were broken off and incorporated into M, where they were trapped as the magma solidified. The inclusions must be older than the rock that contains them.<br><br><b>Marking points (4 marks):</b><br>- 2 marks: the sequence in part (i) in the correct order.<br>- 1 mark: the feature in M identified as inclusions.<br>- 1 mark: the explanation (fragments of N and L broken off and enclosed during the intrusion of M).<br><br>",{});q.figure='<img src="banks/earth-3209/assets/u2-24-aug07-63j.png" alt="Diagram for this question" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">';return q;}]),["Fragments inside a rock are older than the rock around them."]);

