E("es-u2-cr-q18",2,["EA12.11.5"],3,"var",varset([function(){return textq("In the 1950's, scientists determined the age of Earth to be 4.5 billion years old based on uranium/lead dating of meteorites. Canadian scientists at the Geological Survey of Canada initially determined an age of 3.5 billion years old for a rock sample. This age was later found to be incorrect and the rock was actually 2.8 billion years old. Which error most likely occurred during the dating of this rock sample to cause the age to be 3.5 billion years old, and why did this error cause an overestimation of age?",["Lead from the seals of the sample containers contaminated the sample, adding extra daughter isotope; more daughter makes more half-lives appear to have passed, so the calculated age was too old."],"<b>Model answer:</b> The error was contamination by the daughter isotope: lead from the seals of the sample containers entered the sample, raising the measured amount of lead (daughter product). A higher daughter-to-parent ratio makes it appear that more half-lives have passed than actually have, so the calculated age (3.5 billion years) overestimated the true age (2.8 billion years).<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: the error (extra lead from the container seals added to the sample).<br>- 1 mark: the mechanism (added daughter isotope inflates the ratio and the calculated number of half-lives, so the age comes out too old).<br><br>",{});}]),["The date came out too old: which isotope measurement was inflated, and how?"]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: August 2009 public exam, item 61(b).
E("es-u2-cr-q19",2,["EA12.11.5","EA12.2.7"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Using the data below, explain which pair of isotopes could most accurately be used to date an early Precambrian rock sample. Uranium-238 and Lead-206: half-life 4.5 billion years. Uranium-235 and Lead-207: half-life 713 million years. Carbon-14 and Nitrogen-14: half-life 5,730 years.",["Uranium-235 and Lead-207: an early Precambrian rock is about 4.4 billion years old, about 6 half-lives of U-235, leaving measurable parent; C-14's half-life is far too short and U-238's is too long (less than one half-life)."],"<b>Model answer:</b> An early Precambrian rock is approximately 4.4 billion years old. The most accurate pair is uranium-235 and lead-207: dividing 4.4 billion years by the 713-million-year half-life gives about 6 half-lives, leaving a significant, measurable amount of parent isotope. Carbon-14 and nitrogen-14 would not work: the 5,730-year half-life means roughly 767,000 half-lives would have passed, leaving an insignificant amount of carbon-14 to measure. Uranium-238 and lead-206 would also be inaccurate: with a 4.5-billion-year half-life, less than one half-life has passed, so the small amount of decay carries a large error.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: choosing uranium-235 and lead-207.<br>- 1 mark: the explanation, including why at least one other pair fails (C-14 far too short, or U-238 too long for even one half-life).<br><br>Note the official answer rejects the longest half-life: less than one half-life of decay is too little to measure accurately.",{});}]),["A good pair leaves a measurable amount of parent after the rock's whole lifetime."]);

/* ----- Constructed-Response Practice ----- */
// Source: Authored for this bank from the curriculum guide's Labrador zircons STSE elaboration for EA12.7.3.
E("es-u2-cr-q20",2,["EA12.7.3"],2,"var",varset([function(){return textq("Zircon crystals from Labrador are among the oldest materials dated on Earth. In your own words, explain how scientists use radioactive decay in such zircons to establish an absolute age, using appropriate scientific language and conventions.",["Uranium-238 in the zircon decays to lead-206 at a constant half-life of 4.5 billion years; measuring the parent-to-daughter ratio gives the number of half-lives since crystallization, and multiplying gives the age in years."],"<b>Model answer:</b> When a zircon crystallizes, it incorporates uranium but excludes lead, so its clock starts at zero daughter product. The parent isotope uranium-238 then decays to the daughter isotope lead-206 at a constant rate, with a half-life of 4.5 billion years. Measuring the parent-to-daughter ratio in the zircon gives the number of half-lives that have passed since crystallization; multiplying that number by 4.5 billion years gives the absolute age of the zircon.<br><br><b>Marking points (2 marks):</b><br>- 1 mark: decay at a constant, known half-life, with parent and daughter isotopes named correctly.<br>- 1 mark: the ratio converted to a number of half-lives and then to an age stated with units.",{});}]),["Name the parent, the daughter, the half-life, and the step from ratio to age."]);

